<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles VIII of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1470">1470</a>–<a href="/years/1498">1498</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Charles the Affable · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles VIII was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1483, governed in his minority by his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-france">Anne of France</a> and her husband Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>. In 1491 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a>, bringing the duchy under French control. His invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1494 to claim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. He died childless after striking his head on a door lintel at Amboise.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-viii-of-france.jpg" alt="Charles VIII of France" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous / After Jean Perréal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_King_Charles_VIII_of_France_(1470%E2%80%931498),_by_anonymous_artist,_16th_century_(cropped)_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born on 30 June 1470 at the Château d'Amboise, the only surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> and Charlotte of Savoy. He succeeded on 30 August 1483 at the age of 13. His health was poor, and contemporaries thought him pleasant but unsuited to the business of state. As Louis XI wished, the regency went to his elder sister Anne, whom her father called the least foolish woman in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and her husband Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, until 1491. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mad-war">Mad War</a> of 1485 to 1488 the great lords rebelled against royal centralisation, and the royal government won.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Charles had been betrothed in 1483 to the three-year-old Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, daughter of Maximilian of Austria. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>, Duke of Brittany, died in 1488, his daughter Anne, aged 11, inherited the duchy and in 1490 married Maximilian by proxy. The regents refused a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> on a second French border, and the French army invaded Brittany while Maximilian was preoccupied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Anne of Brittany was forced to renounce Maximilian and marry Charles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langeais">Langeais</a> in December 1491. Margaret went home in 1493 with her dowry, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> was retained under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a>.</p><p>Charles bought the neutrality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Aragon and Maximilian by the treaties of Étaples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a> in 1492 and 1493, and built a large army with one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s first siege trains. He invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in September 1494 with 25,000 men, 8,000 of them Swiss mercenaries, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> without a pitched battle on 22 February 1495 and was crowned King of Naples on 20 May. The Italian rulers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the Empire formed the League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> on 31 March 1495, but could not stop him at Fornovo in July from marching home, and Aragonese forces soon subdued his garrisons in Naples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles died at Amboise in 1498. By the usual account he struck his head on a door lintel on the way to a game of real tennis and died nine hours later; a study of 2021 held that such a blow would cause only minor injury. All his children died before him, so he was the last of the elder branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a>, and Louis XII succeeded him. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in debt, but his expedition strengthened cultural ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and energised French art and literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles VIII of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Her proxy marriage to Maximilian of Austria in 1490 brought French armies back; besieged in Rennes, Anne agreed to marry <b>Charles VIII of France</b>, and the wedding took place at Langeais in December 1491.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles VIII of France</b> entered the city in February 1495 almost unopposed but soon withdrew; Louis XII and Ferdinand II of Aragon agreed in 1500 to partition the kingdom, then fell out, and the Spanish commander Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba, the 'Great Captain', won the Battle of Cerignola,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Charles VIII of France</b> invaded Italy in 1494, Piero de’ Medici surrendered fortresses to him and was expelled by the citizens, and the friar Girolamo Savonarola dominated the restored republic until he was burned in 1498.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Charles VIII of France</b> invaded Italy in 1494, his mobile siege guns brought down in hours walls that had stood for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>She went on to marry <b>Charles VIII of France</b> in 1491 and Louis XII in 1499, twice becoming queen of France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-19">19 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Anne of France and her husband Peter II governed the kingdom during the minority of <b>Charles VIII</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bourbon">Duchy of Bourbon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Amboise, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Amboise, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.41,0.98&amp;year=1488" aria-label="Amboise on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles VIII of France"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5057 6068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5057 6068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.41,0.98&amp;year=1488">What was here in 1488? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
